Fix a buffer overflow in ps when a number is wider than its column.
The line buffer is sized from the column widths, but pid, ppid and the
other numeric columns are printed with "%*u", which writes the whole
number however narrow the column. A column narrowed by a short header,
as in "ps -o pid=X", wrote past the end of the buffer, and with several
such columns the output came out garbled. PIDs above 99999 overflow
the default five-column width too. The padding after such a value also
went negative, which printf takes as left alignment, so it wrote
extra spaces past the value as well.

Reserve room for a full number in every column, and pad a value that
overflows its column with a single space.

---
 procps/ps.c |    6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/procps/ps.c
+++ b/procps/ps.c
@@ -504,7 +504,9 @@
 	if (!is_selinux_enabled())
 		need_flags &= ~PSSCAN_CONTEXT;
 #endif
-	buffer = xmalloc(width + 1); /* for trailing \0 */
+	/* Numbers are not cut to the field width, so each field
+	 * may take up to sizeof(int)*3 chars more */
+	buffer = xmalloc(width + out_cnt * sizeof(int)*3 + 1); /* for trailing \0 */
 }
 
 static void format_header(void)
@@ -546,6 +548,8 @@
 		len = strlen(p);
 		p += len;
 		len = out[i].width - len + 1;
+		if (len < 1) /* value was wider than the field */
+			len = 1;
 		if (++i == out_cnt) /* do not pad last field */
 			break;
 		p += sprintf(p, "%*s", len, " "); /* " ", not "", to ensure separation of fields */
